INT. LIGHTHOUSE KEEPER'S COTTAGE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

A pot of tea between them on a scarred table. Oil lamp. Rain on the glass.

INSPECTOR WILHELMINA CROSS, fifties, sits with her coat still on. Across from her, a YOUNG MAN in a fisherman's jersey, hands round his cup.

CROSS
You said you were alone that night.

YOUNG MAN
I was.

CROSS
The log says three lamps were lit.

YOUNG MAN
I lit one.

CROSS
Then who lit the other two?

YOUNG MAN
The wind, maybe.

CROSS
Lamps don't catch on wind.

YOUNG MAN
Then I don't know.

CROSS
Mr. Penhallow. I've been doing this a long time. In my experience, a man who says "I don't know" three times in a row knows exactly.

YOUNG MAN
I'm not Penhallow.

CROSS
No?

YOUNG MAN
Penhallow's my uncle. He's the keeper. I'm Tobias. I came up on the supply boat on Tuesday.

CROSS
(after a long pause)
Tobias.

TOBIAS
Yes.

CROSS
Then where is your uncle, Tobias?

TOBIAS
Out on the rocks. He goes every night. To watch for ships.

CROSS
There are no ships tonight.

TOBIAS
He doesn't believe that.

CROSS
And the third lamp?

TOBIAS
That's for the ones who don't come back.

A beat. Rain. The kettle ticks.

CROSS
Tobias. I think you should tell me everything.
